  "output": "{\"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nParsing is the slow part of `logslice` on big files (the 1.2 GB ingest log from the nightly job takes ~40 s just to parse). This PR adds an opt-in on-disk cache of parsed records, so repeated queries against the same file skip parsing entirely.\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- New `--cache` flag. When set, parsed records are loaded from the cache if present; otherwise the file is parsed and the result is saved for next time.\\n- New module `logslice/cache.py` with `load()` / `store()`.\\n- Cache entries are stored as JSON under `~/.cache/logslice/`, one file per log file.\\n- The cache key is computed by `_key()` from the file\u0027s resolved path, size and mtime, so editing or appending to a log automatically invalidates its entry.\\n- Cache entries never expire. Since the key changes whenever the file changes, there is no need for a TTL; delete `~/.cache/logslice/` to clear the cache.\\n- `--cache` is ignored (with a warning) when reading from stdin.\\n- README: new \\\"Caching\\\" section.\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\n- `pytest`: new `tests/test_cache.py` covers the store/load round trip, key changes on modification, and corrupt entries; `tests/test_cli.py` covers the `--cache` hit path and that nothing is written without the flag.\\n- Manually: `logslice --cache --count-by level ingest.log` twice; the second run went from 41 s to 0.4 s.\\n\"}\n"
}

